# Protection for the English checkout API directory.
#
# This directory sits in a document root fed by a PUBLIC git mirror, so the
# code here is world-readable by design. The credentials are not: _secrets.php
# is uploaded out of band and must never be served, listed or downloaded.

Options -Indexes

# Any file starting with an underscore is internal: the secrets file, the
# shared library, and the log directory. None of them are endpoints.
<FilesMatch "^_">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Belt and braces for the logs, which contain customer e-mail addresses.
<FilesMatch "\.(log|json)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# ── RETIRED: the English custom checkout (2026-08-03) ────────────────────────
#
# The English business moved to its own WordPress on vip.genmarketer.eu, which
# sells natively through WooCommerce + WCS + Stripe. These endpoints are the
# previous, bespoke channel. Nothing links to them any more — but they were
# still answering, still holding LIVE Stripe credentials, and their WordPress
# bridge on tudastar.genmarketer.hu is gone (gm-en-checkout deactivated), so a
# payment started here could no longer become an order. Money in, nothing out:
# that is the reason this block exists and why it is a block, not a cleanup.
#
# Denied here rather than deleted-and-hoped, because this directory is served
# from TWO document roots — ai-csapat.genmarketer.hu (a git clone, where a file
# deletion does propagate) and genmarketer.eu (uploaded over TUS, where it does
# NOT: the uploader has no delete). One file, both roots, same day.
#
# The Hungarian bespoke checkout is deliberately NOT in this list. It is
# switched off at the application level (hu-health: funnel_open=false) but its
# chain is intact and Károly kept it as a fallback (2026-08-03).
#
# Anchored on ^ so that hu-create-intent.php and friends never match.
#
# ── The SIGNAL matters, not just the block (2026-08-04) ─────────────────────
#
# `Require all denied` answers 403 Forbidden, which means "you may not" — a
# permission problem, and permission problems are the kind a client retries.
# What is true here is stronger and permanent: these endpoints are GONE. The
# English business no longer sells through them and never will again.
#
# 410 says exactly that, and it is the one status code a well-behaved client
# treats as final: crawlers drop the URL instead of re-checking it, and a stale
# integration stops retrying instead of hammering a dead payment endpoint
# forever. With 403 the two cases are indistinguishable from the outside.
#
# mod_rewrite's [G] runs in the fixup phase, BEFORE authz, so it wins — and the
# `Require all denied` below stays as belt and braces: if mod_rewrite were ever
# unavailable, the endpoints still do not answer. Never weaken that to a bare
# redirect: the deny is what guarantees the PHP is not executed.
#
# The pattern is relative to this directory (per-directory .htaccess), so it
# does not assume where `api/` sits in either of the two document roots.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteRule ^(create-intent|create-subscription|stripe-webhook|payment-status|capture-lead|portal|portal-link|replay|health)\.php$ - [G,L]
</IfModule>

<FilesMatch "^(create-intent|create-subscription|stripe-webhook|payment-status|capture-lead|portal|portal-link|replay|health)\.php$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Never cache an API response.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set Cache-Control "no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate"
  Header set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
</IfModule>

# LiteSpeed's full-page cache would happily serve one customer's JSON to the
# next one. It is disabled for the whole site, but this directory is where it
# would hurt most, so it is stated explicitly.
<IfModule LiteSpeed>
  CacheDisable public /
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteRule .* - [E=Cache-Control:no-cache]
</IfModule>
